+\title{Driven Kerr Cavity in Bargmann Space}
+\author{\TODO{Authors}}
+\date{\today}
+
+\begin{document}
+
+\maketitle
+
+\begin{abstract}
+We study the stationary eigenproblem of a coherently driven single-mode Kerr
+cavity in the Bargmann representation.  The bosonic problem is converted into
+a second-order differential equation for an entire function and an associated
+three-term recurrence.  This formulation is intended to support a spectral
+condition that avoids direct Hamiltonian diagonalization and a controlled
+evaluation of the wavefunction throughout the complex plane.  We outline the
+analysis of zeros, growth, amplitude and phase landscapes, and distinguish it
+from the exploratory complex dynamics generated by iterating the Bargmann
+wavefunction.
+\end{abstract}
+
+\section{Introduction}
+
+A coherently driven nonlinear cavity is a basic model in quantum optics.  Its
+Kerr nonlinearity makes the stationary problem nontrivial while its single-mode
+structure permits a detailed complex-analytic treatment.  The Bargmann
+representation is especially natural: state vectors become entire functions,
+creation becomes multiplication, and annihilation becomes differentiation.
+
+Our aims are to (i) obtain a non-diagonalization-based spectral condition,
+(ii) evaluate the resulting entire eigenfunctions with explicit truncation
+control, and (iii) characterize their zeros and amplitude--phase geometry on
+the complex plane.  Iteration of an eigenfunction as a complex map is treated
+separately as an exploratory visualization rather than as physical time
+evolution.
+
+\section{Model and conventions}
+\label{sec:model}
+
+In a frame rotating at the drive frequency and with $\hbar=1$, we consider
+\begin{equation}
+  H = \omega_0 N + \frac{V}{2}N(N-1)
+      + F a^\dagger + \overline F a,
+  \qquad N=a^\dagger a,
+  \label{eq:hamiltonian}
+\end{equation}
+where $\omega_0\in\mathbb R$ is the detuning coefficient in the convention of
+\cref{eq:hamiltonian}, $V>0$ is the Kerr coefficient, and $F\in\C$ is the
+coherent-drive amplitude.  Thus the two linear terms are mutual adjoints.  A
+phase rotation of the mode can make $F$ real and non-negative, but we retain the
+complex notation to keep self-adjointness explicit.  We address the closed
+stationary problem
+\begin{equation}
+  H\lvert\psi\rangle=E\lvert\psi\rangle.
+\end{equation}
+The relation to a dissipative steady state governed by a Lindblad equation is
+outside the present spectral problem and will be discussed separately if
+needed.
+
+Let $\mathcal H=\ell^2(\mathbb N_0)$ be the one-mode Fock space, let
+$\{\lvert n\rangle\}_{n\geq0}$ be its number basis, and let
+$\mathcal D_{\rm fin}=\operatorname{span}\{\lvert n\rangle:n\geq0\}$.  Define
+\begin{equation}
+  H_0=\omega_0N+\frac V2N(N-1),
+  \qquad
+  \mathcal D(H_0)=\mathcal D(N^2)
+  =\left\{u:\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}n^4|u_n|^2<\infty\right\}.
+  \label{eq:free-domain}
+\end{equation}
+The diagonal operator $H_0$ is self-adjoint on this domain and essentially
+self-adjoint on $\mathcal D_{\rm fin}$.  Moreover, since its eigenvalues grow
+as $Vn^2/2$, the graph norms of $H_0$ and $N^2$ are equivalent.  The standard
+estimates
+\begin{equation}
+  \norm{a u}^2=\langle u,Nu\rangle,
+  \qquad
+  \norm{a^\dagger u}^2=\langle u,(N+1)u\rangle
+\end{equation}
+and the scalar inequality $\sqrt{n+1}\leq\varepsilon n^2+C_\varepsilon$
+show that $F a^\dagger+\overline F a$ is infinitesimally $H_0$-bounded.
+Kato--Rellich therefore gives a self-adjoint operator $H$ on
+$\mathcal D(H)=\mathcal D(N^2)$.  Equivalence of the perturbed and unperturbed
+graph norms also shows that $\mathcal D_{\rm fin}$ is a core for $H$; hence the
+restriction of $H$ to $\mathcal D_{\rm fin}$ is essentially self-adjoint.
+
+These estimates also settle the basic spectral geometry.  For suitable
+constants $C_0,C_1<\infty$,
+\begin{equation}
+  H_0\geq \frac V4N^2-C_0,
+  \qquad
+  F a^\dagger+\overline F a\geq-\frac V8N^2-C_1
+  \label{eq:lower-bound-estimates}
+\end{equation}
+as quadratic-form inequalities on $\mathcal D(N^2)$.  Consequently $H$ is
+bounded from below for every $\omega_0\in\mathbb R$ and $F\in\C$ when $V>0$.
+Finally, $(H_0-z)^{-1}$ is compact because its diagonal entries tend to zero.
+For a common resolvent point $z$ with sufficiently large negative real part,
+the resolvent identity, together with the boundedness of
+$(F a^\dagger+\overline F a)(H_0-z)^{-1}$, implies that $(H-z)^{-1}$ is
+compact.  Thus $H$ has purely discrete spectrum, consisting of finite-
+multiplicity eigenvalues accumulating only at $+\infty$.
+
+\section{Bargmann formulation}
+\label{sec:bargmann}
+
+The Bargmann--Fock space consists of entire functions with norm
+\begin{equation}
+  \norm{\psi}_{\mathcal B}^{2}
+  = \frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\C}|\psi(z)|^2e^{-|z|^2}\,\dd^2z < \infty.
+\end{equation}
+Under the correspondence
+\begin{equation}
+  a^\dagger\mapsto z,
+  \qquad
+  a\mapsto \partial_z,
+\end{equation}
+the eigenvalue equation becomes
+\begin{equation}
+  \frac{V}{2}z^2\psi''(z)
+  + (\omega_0 z+\overline F)\psi'(z)
+  + (Fz-E)\psi(z)=0.
+  \label{eq:bargmann-ode}
+\end{equation}
+Writing $\psi(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}c_nz^n$, with $c_{-1}=0$, yields
+\begin{equation}
+  \overline F(n+1)c_{n+1}
+  + \left[\frac{V}{2}n(n-1)+\omega_0 n-E\right]c_n
+  + Fc_{n-1}=0,
+  \qquad n\geq0.
+  \label{eq:recurrence}
+\end{equation}
+The physical normalization condition is
+$\sum_{n\geq0}n!\,|c_n|^2<\infty$.
+An entire solution of \cref{eq:bargmann-ode} is therefore only a formal or
+analytic candidate: it represents a vector of $\mathcal H$ precisely when this
+weighted coefficient sum is finite, and it represents an eigenvector of $H$
+only when it also belongs to $\mathcal D(N^2)$ and satisfies the equation in
+the Hilbert-space sense.  For an eigenvector the latter domain condition is
+automatic from self-adjointness, but it must not be inferred from entireness
+alone.
+
+\section{Spectral condition}
+\label{sec:spectrum}
+
+For $F\neq0$, \cref{eq:recurrence} is a three-term recurrence and its $n=0$
+equation fixes $c_1$ in terms of $c_0$.  Bargmann normalizability supplies an
+additional global condition on the resulting sequence.  It is plausible that
+this condition can be expressed by matching the solution initialized at
+$n=0$ to a minimal large-$n$ solution, but neither existence and uniqueness of
+such a minimal solution nor convergence of the associated continued fraction
+has yet been proved.  We therefore do not yet define a spectral determinant or
+claim an equivalence between its zeros and the spectrum.
+
+The case $F=0$ is exceptional: the recurrence decouples and the number states
+give the eigenvalues $E_n=\omega_0n+Vn(n-1)/2$.  Any later continued-fraction
+construction must either assume $F\neq0$ or treat this limit separately.
